209:
The following classes of licenses shall be authorized:
(1) Malt beverage:
a. Wholesale;
b. Retail package;
c. Consumption on the premises;
d. Combination sales with wine and/or distilled spirits;
e. Brewpub;
f. Brewery;
g. Microbrewery.
(2) Wine:
a. Wholesale;
b. Retail package;
c. Consumption on the premises;
d. Combination sales with malt beverages and/or distilled spirits;
e. Microwinery.
(3) Distilled spirits:
a. Wholesale;
b. Retail package;
c. Consumption on the premises;
d. Combination sales with malt beverages and/or wine;
e. Microdistillery.
(4) Temporary off-premises and special event.
(5) Sunday sales permit.
(6) Ancillary tasting license. Upon filing an application and payment of a filing fee and a license fee and after a background investigation by the police department and review by the City Clerk, an annual license may be issued to a licensed retail package wine, distilled spirits or malt beverage dealer or licensed wine, distilled spirits or malt beverage wholesaler to hold wine, distilled spirits and/or malt beverage tastings, or a wholly owned subsidiary of a licensed package wine, distilled spirits or malt beverage dealer or licensed wine, distilled spirits or malt beverage wholesaler, respectively. The license shall allow the applicant to provide samples of wine, distilled spirits and/or malt beverage to the public for consumption at a location which meets legal requirements for on-premises consumption, with the exception of a full-service kitchen requirement, under the following conditions:
a. The applicant for a wine, distilled spirits and/or malt beverage tasting license must hold any and all applicable licenses and permits required by the state and the city, including, but not limited to, those named above.
b. All federal, state, and local laws and restrictions that apply to wine tastings shall also apply to malt beverage and distilled spirits tastings.
c. The application shall be accompanied by the requisite fee in an amount as set by resolution of the city council, which amount shall remain in effect until modified or amended by subsequent resolutions adopted by the city council. The fee shall be the same for wine, distilled spirits and malt beverage tasting licenses.
d. Should a single applicant apply for more than one (1) tasting license in the same calendar year, the applicant shall be subject to and have to pay for only one (1) police background investigation. However, the applicant would be responsible for paying the license fee for each application.
e. Subject to the requirements of subsection (f) below, wine and malt beverage tasting shall be conducted at a wine or malt beverage counter area constituting no more than ten (10) percent of the entire floor area of the premises or in a separate "Tasting Room" separated from the sale floor. Distilled spirits tasting may only be conducted in a separate "tasting room" separated from the sale floor.
f. An establishment licensed to sell package distilled spirits may conduct tastings only in a separate "tasting room" immediately adjacent to and completely separated from the sales floor and having a separate public entrance, occupation tax certificate and trade name from the retail package establishment and must otherwise comply with Georgia Department of Revenue Regulation 560-2-3-14, and as it may be amended in the future. The separate occupation tax certificate and trade name may be owned by a wholly-owned subsidiary of the owner of the adjacent retail package licensee.
g. Samples of malt beverages shall not exceed sixteen (16) ounces, one (1) ounce for distilled spirits and twelve (12) ounces for samples of wine, and no customer shall consume more than thirty-two (32) ounces of a malt beverage, three (3) ounces of distilled spirits or twelve (12) ounces of wine samples per one day. The licensee may charge customers for any samples.
(Ord. No. 2010-31, § 1, 11-15-10; Ord. No. 2015-02, § 1, 3-2-15; Ord. No. 2018-012, § II, 8-20-18)

218:
(a) Any applicant for an alcoholic beverage license or employee permit shall give written permission or get permission from any person, as needed, to the City and execute such other documentation as the City may require to acquire a five-year criminal history on any person necessary in the processing of the application. The applicant shall prepay all costs incidental thereto or make such initial deposit as may be required so the City may obtain a criminal history for a five-year period prior to the date of such application. The criminal history may be in any of the following forms or combinations thereof, but the histories must cover the entire required five (5) years:
(1) An NCIC report certified by a federal or state law enforcement agency;
(2) A GCIC report if the applicant has resided in the State of Georgia for any portion of the ten-year period (or a similar report of other state agencies if the applicant resided in other states during the ten-year period);
(3) First offender status will be recognized only upon satisfactory completion of the terms of any sentence which may be imposed.
(b) Each application for a license under this chapter shall be accompanied by a certified check or cash for the full amount of the license fee, together with a separate certified check or cash in an amount determined by the City Council and listed in the schedule of fees and charges maintained in the City Clerk's office to defray investigative and administrative costs. If the application is denied and the license refused, or if the applicant withdraws his application prior to a decision of the City Clerk's decision to approve or deny same, the deposit representing the license fee shall be refunded but the fee paid for investigation shall be retained.
(c) (1) No person shall be granted an alcoholic beverage license unless it shall appear to the satisfaction of the City Council that such person, partners in the firm, officers and directors of the corporation and any other party with a financial interest in the business or prospective permittee, shall be of good moral character. In no event shall any license be granted to any person who has been convicted of or has pled guilty to or entered a plea of nolo contendere to any felony involving the following offenses within a period of five (5) years immediately prior to the filing of such application:
a. Crimes of moral turpitude;
b. Lottery law violations;
c. Gambling;
d. Gaming violations;
e. Felony possession, distribution or sale of illegal narcotics or alcoholic beverages;
f. Pandering, solicitation for lewd purposes, prostitution or other sex related offenses;
g. Tax offenses;
h. Financial transaction card fraud;
i. Forgery;
j. Identity theft.
(2) In no event shall any license be granted to any person who has been convicted of or has pled guilty to or entered a plea of nolo contendere to any misdemeanor involving the following offenses within a period of two (2) years immediately prior to the filing of such application:
a. The offenses listed in subsections (c)(1)a—g, above;
b. Assault, battery or other crimes of violence;
c. Theft, embezzlement, extortion;
(d) (1) No person shall be granted an employee permit unless it shall appear to the satisfaction of the City Council that such person shall be of good moral character. In no event shall any employee permit be granted to any person who has been convicted of or has pled guilty to or entered a plea of nolo contendere to any felony involving the following offenses within a period of five (5) years immediately prior to the filing of such application:
a. Crimes of moral turpitude;
b. Financial transaction card fraud;
c. Forgery;
d. Identity theft.
(2) In no event shall any employee permit be granted to any person who has been convicted of or has pled guilty to or entered a plea of nolo contendere to any misdemeanor involving the following offenses within a period of two (2) years immediately prior to the filing of such application:
a. The offenses listed in subsections (d)(1)a—d, above;
b. Assault, battery or other crimes of violence;
c. Theft, embezzlement, extortion;
(e) Consideration of the offenses listed in subsections (c) and (d) are deemed to be reasonable, clearly articulated and ascertainable standards which are substantially related to the business of alcoholic beverage sales, handling, manufacturing and distribution, and are connected to the regulatory purpose of the license. Convictions or pleas of nolo contendere to the offenses listed in [subsections] (c) and (d) above are deemed to indicate that the applicant will not maintain and utilize the operation or permit which he is seeking in conformity with federal, state, local laws, rules and regulations.
(f) At the time an application is submitted for an alcoholic beverage license or employee permit, the applicant shall, by a duly sworn or affirmed affidavit, certify that neither he nor any of the other owners of the establishment, nor the manager nor any agent of such establishment has been convicted or has pleaded guilty or entered a plea of nolo contendere to any of the offenses under the timelines provided in subsections (c) and (d).
(g) A license application or permit may be denied where it appears that the applicant would not have adequate participation in the proposed business to direct and manage its affairs, or where it appears that the applicant is intended to be a mere surrogate for a person or persons who would not otherwise qualify for a license for any reason whatsoever.
(h) The written application for the license shall be a permanent record which the licensee must maintain current as required by this chapter. The applicant shall have the duty to keep said application current until denied or abandoned. Failure to maintain a current application shall be grounds for revocation of the license or permit.
(i) No person shall have, own, or enjoy any ownership or interest in, share in the profits from, or otherwise participate in the business of any alcoholic beverage licensee in the City unless a full description of such interest shall have been furnished the City Clerk either in the application or at the time such interest may arise. It shall be the duty of the licensee to report to the Clerk, within five (5) days, any change in any interest in such licensee's business, including, but not limited to, any division of the profits, division of net or gross sales for any purpose whatsoever, change in the payment of rents or leases, change in the ownership of any lease or building or land used in such business, or change in the ownership of any corporation that has any interest in such business or the change of management of such corporation. For any entity which issues shares of stock, the change of ownership reporting requirement detailed herein shall not be effectuated until any person holds more than twenty (20) percent of the issued stock.
(1) After receipt of such notice, the City Clerk shall notify such person within a reasonable time of any objection to the ownership interest set forth therein, and it shall then be incumbent upon such person to dispose of such interest within thirty (30) days after the mailing of the notice to do so by the City Clerk to such person at his address as shown on such notice of interest. In the event such interest is not disposed of within thirty (30) days after the mailing of the notice by the City Clerk, or in the event no objection is filed within such thirty-day period, or in the event such person fails to notify the City Clerk of the transfer of any such interest within twenty (20) days after the acquisition thereof, then the license, as provided for herein, may be suspended or revoked in accordance with the provisions of section 3-47 until disposition of the interest has been made or notice given to the City Clerk of the transfer.
(2) The report required by this section shall be in the form required by the City Clerk and shall be an amendment to the licensee's license application on file with the City Clerk and as such, shall be under oath or affirmation and verified as otherwise required by license applications.
(j) The license issued shall be valid only for the calendar year indicated thereon. A licensee that desires to continue business during the next or subsequent calendar year must make a renewal application for such year on or before December 31 of the preceding year. Payment of license fees for renewal of a license may be made at the time of application and upon approval of renewal by the City Clerk and payment, the license will be issued. Renewals are new applications. There shall be no protected property interest in any license. The City Clerk may use a modified application that allows reports of changes in renewals rather than a full application. Changes in measured distances shall be reported in accordance with section 3-17.
(k) All licenses issued pursuant to this chapter shall be valid only so long as the licensee is actively engaged in such business, with the exception of federally recognized holidays, vacations of the resident agent not exceeding thirty (30), and periods of repair, redecoration or remodeling, not exceeding sixty (60) days in a single calendar year where three (3) days' written notice has been given in advance of such repair, redecoration, remodeling or vacation, is given to the City Clerk, the business shall be continuously opened as set out in this chapter, and in the event the licensee shall cease to be actively engaged in such business, such license shall be invalid and the licensee of such business shall immediately notify the City Clerk and return his license thereto.
(l) The City Clerk shall require a personal financial statement of any applicant for licensing purposes but not employment permitting.
(Ord. No. 2010-31, § 1, 11-15-10; Ord. No. 2012-03, § 2, 1-17-12; Ord. No. 2020-013, § 2, 8-19-20)
